WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.760
This is a very simple test.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:04.560
One thing will change in this&nbsp;
photo. And one thing only.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.920
Try to spot the change.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:15.920
I’ll give you a few seconds to figure it out.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.200
Here’s what changed.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.880
See the clay pots on the table?

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.760
They’re brown now. Before, they were grey.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.990
But if you didn’t notice, don’t worry, you’re not&nbsp;
alone. Most people don’t notice what’s right in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:30.320
front of them. Even if they think they would.   
 
That’s the part I find most interesting. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.000
people think they’ll notice things that&nbsp;
they actually don’t. You can make changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.360
that are fairly big, even bigger than the&nbsp;
one you made, and people will miss those.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.080
Psychology professor Dan Simons of the&nbsp;
University of Illinois is famous for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.640
experiment where people were so busy counting the&nbsp;
times those dressed in white passed a basketball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.160
that they failed to see the&nbsp;
gorilla walk past in plain sight!

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.360
Why is it that we fail to see things&nbsp;
that are right in front of us?

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.720
Part of it is just we have limits on how&nbsp;
much we can focus on and pay attention to.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.960
If the visible structure of the eye is&nbsp;
like a camera lens, then the retina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.440
the inner layer of tissue at the back of&nbsp;
the eye, is like the film inside the camera.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.560
This delicate organ forms an image by collecting&nbsp;
light reflected from the surrounding environment.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.720
But within the retina, very little&nbsp;
of it is actually “high-resolution”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.280
consisting of “fovea” that does&nbsp;
complicated tasks like identifying faces.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.840
Most of our vision is low resolution.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.360
So we can’t see everything.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.000
We are bombarded by so many stimuli that&nbsp;
it exceeds the capacity of our brain.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
So we ignore most of what we “see”.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.280
If you think the clay pot example was&nbsp;
too subtle, you’re about to see one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.640
of Dr. Simons’ experiments showing an EXTREME&nbsp;
example of how much some of us fail to notice.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.760
An unsuspecting pedestrian is&nbsp;
giving directions to the guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.040
on the left who is actually one of&nbsp;
the experimenters in this study.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.760
A door passes between them, and the first&nbsp;
experimenter is replaced by someone else.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.640
And the man giving directions has no clue&nbsp;
he’s now talking to a different person!

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.840
Eight of the 15 people approached in&nbsp;
the study didn’t know the person they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.160
were talking to was replaced with someone else.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:19.280
After the change we’ll say hey, did you&nbsp;
notice anything unusual? It’s kind of rude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.800
they could have gone around us. They really&nbsp;
had no idea they were talking to someone else.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.080
And he says those who did notice the&nbsp;
person changed kept giving directions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.120
anyway because they felt the&nbsp;
social pressure to do that. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.480
what are the implications of&nbsp;
change blindness in everyday life?

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.960
If you forget who your waiter is, that’s natural.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.120
It would be easier to remember if something&nbsp;
about them sticks out and grabs your attention.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.480
Like if they had blue hair.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.600
Our failure to see change is why we tend&nbsp;
not to notice continuity errors in films.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.280
If you’re patting yourself on the back thinking&nbsp;
you’re great at noticing mistakes in movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.880
well, here’s some news for you.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.320
Clip People who notice them are convinced they&nbsp;
always notice them. But of course, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.240
only aware of the ones they happened to notice.&nbsp;
They’re not aware of all the ones they missed!

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.240
There’s another more serious real-life&nbsp;
implication of change blindness.  
&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.000
It could explain why eyewitness&nbsp;
testimony may sometimes be unreliable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.120
leading to a witness wrongly&nbsp;
identifying a suspect.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.040
So, now that you’re more aware of how&nbsp;
unaware you are, time for another test.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.200
You’re about to see two images&nbsp;
switching back and forth rapidly.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.960
Keep your gaze fixed on the center.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:31.840
And see if you can spot the&nbsp;
difference between them.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.640
So, did you see what changed?

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.160
If not, pay attention to the man&nbsp;
on the right wearing the jeans.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.880
But just like before: keep&nbsp;
your gaze on the center so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.840
that only your attention has moved, not your gaze.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.920
Did you catch his jeans changing&nbsp;
color from blue to brown?

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.280
Now that you know what’s changing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.640
you can see the change happening for&nbsp;
yourself because you’re focused on it.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.400
If you don’t focus on what’s changing&nbsp;
you're simply not going to see the change.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.400
How about one more example?

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.240
Did you notice what changed this time?

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.080
Take a look at the boat on&nbsp;
the right. It has disappeared.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.240
Seeing is difficult. Because we are limited&nbsp;
in our sight, we have to let so much go.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.560
There are some life lessons here, too.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.942
Perhaps what you truly need is in front of you.

00:04:29.942 --> 00:04:33.542
 
But you can’t see it because you’re
focused on something else. 

00:04:33.542 --> 00:04:39.091
In other words, you may not&nbsp;
have seen what you truly need.

00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:41.812
I hope you’ve enjoyed this video.

00:04:41.812 --> 00:04:46.441
So does this make you better at detecting&nbsp;
changes? Maybe. Maybe not. For example,

00:04:46.441 --> 00:04:49.623
did you notice that my shirt changed
throughout this video?

00:04:49.623 --> 00:04:51.223
Leave a comment if you did.

00:04:51.223 --> 00:04:55.012
Turns out, you can get away&nbsp;
with a lot more than you think.

00:04:55.012 --> 00:04:58.292
Awareness is tricky to improve unless&nbsp;
you know what you’re working for.

